  ('zcrm_1579374000168672008', 'contact', 'zcrm_1579374000168804002', 'James Vardas', NULL, '7-23-25 James was laid off from his work in biologics March 28, 2025. He doesn''t have any insurance at the moment and missed the SEP. He has a knee issue he thinks may need to be scoped. Takes aciclovir on occasion which looks to be super affordable. He just signed a new contract for work starting soon. His income is typically around $400K. He''s getting severance through Aug. Since he can''t join C4 and won''t qualify for LifeX, Premium HSA is his best solution. He understands the pre-x clause. I explained the nuances for Premium HSA for pre-x and how Needs are paid. His daughter Claire 11-17-2006 needs proof of ins for Aug. 1st ideally for CU.  His son Emmet 4/26/2009 also needs ins. His ex-wife Claire earns about $160K but they swap which kid they cover for taxes every year. Either way I run the numbers, Molly also doesn''t qualify for a tax credit in 2025. So, she can put Claire on her ins that she chooses, to appease CU. James can put both kids onto his membership since cost is the same for one or two kids. Premium HSA is best since he does annual wellness screenings and he can take advantage of HSA, $1000/$676 or James solo is $1000/$363.  He can get a Sept. 1st start date.', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', 'Wendy Scipione - SR VP National Sales Advisor', '2025-07-23 14:07:35', '2025-07-23 14:07:35'),
